  "authoredStories": [
    {
      "source": "content/people/stories/rulers-early.json",
      "story": {
        "short": "A Levite child saved from the river, raised as Pharaoh's daughter's son, who met God at the bush and led Israel to the edge of the promised land.",
        "paragraphs": [
          {
            "text": "Moses was born in Egypt after Pharaoh had ordered that every son born should be cast into the river. His parents were Levites; later lists name them Amram and Jochebed, with Aaron and Miriam as his brother and sister. His mother hid him for three months, then laid him in a basket of bulrushes among the reeds while his sister watched. Pharaoh's daughter found him, and his sister fetched their mother, who was paid to nurse him. When the child grew, he became Pharaoh's daughter's son, and she named him Moses: \"Because I drew him out of the water.\"",
            "refs": [
              [
                2001022,
                2001022
              ],
              [
                2002001,
                2002010
              ],
              [
                2006020,
                2006020
              ],
              [
                4026059,
                4026059
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "The book of Acts says Moses \"was learned in all the wisdom of the Egyptians\". When he was grown, forty years old by Acts' count, he saw an Egyptian beating a Hebrew and killed the Egyptian. The next day a Hebrew asked him, \"Who made thee a prince and a judge over us?\" Pharaoh heard of it and sought to kill him, and Moses fled to Midian. There he helped the seven daughters of the priest Reuel at a well, married one of them, Zipporah, and named their son Gershom: \"I have been a stranger in a strange land.\"",
            "refs": [
              [
                44007022,
                44007029
              ],
              [
                2002011,
                2002022
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "Forty years later, Acts says, while he was keeping the flock of his father-in-law Jethro near Horeb, Moses saw a bush that burned but was not consumed. God spoke to him from it and sent him to bring Israel out of Egypt. Moses held back, saying he was \"slow of speech\", and God gave him Aaron to speak for him. On the road back to Egypt the LORD met Moses and sought to kill him, and Zipporah circumcised their son. Moses was eighty when he and Aaron stood before Pharaoh.",
            "refs": [
              [
                44007030,
                44007030
              ],
              [
                2003001,
                2003010
              ],
              [
                2004010,
                2004016
              ],
              [
                2004024,
                2004026
              ],
              [
                2007007,
                2007007
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "The forty years in which he led Israel out of Egypt and through the wilderness are told on his page as a leader. Exodus says that \"the LORD spake unto Moses face to face, as a man speaketh unto his friend.\" When he came down from Sinai with the tablets, the skin of his face shone and he did not know it, and the people were afraid to come near him. When Israel made the golden calf, he broke the tablets, and then pleaded for the people, asking to be blotted out of God's book if they could not be forgiven.",
            "refs": [
              [
                44007036,
                44007036
              ],
              [
                2033011,
                2033011
              ],
              [
                2034029,
                2034030
              ],
              [
                2032019,
                2032019
              ],
              [
                2032031,
                2032032
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "Jethro brought Zipporah and her two sons, Gershom and Eliezer, to Moses in the wilderness at the mount of God. Later Miriam and Aaron spoke against Moses because of the Ethiopian woman he had married, and asked whether the LORD had spoken only by him. The text adds that \"the man Moses was very meek, above all the men which were upon the face of the earth.\" When Miriam was struck with leprosy, Moses cried, \"Heal her now, O God, I beseech thee.\" Chronicles says his sons were counted with the tribe of Levi.",
            "refs": [
              [
                2018002,
                2018006
              ],
              [
                4012001,
                4012003
              ],
              [
                4012010,
                4012013
              ],
              [
                13023014,
                13023015
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "At Meribah, told to speak to the rock, Moses struck it twice with his rod, and God told him he would not bring the people into the land. Miriam died at Kadesh and Aaron on mount Hor. Moses begged to cross the Jordan and was told, \"Let it suffice thee\". From the top of Pisgah he saw the land, and he died in Moab at a hundred and twenty, \"his eye was not dim, nor his natural force abated\"; no one knows where his grave is. Israel wept for him thirty days, and Matthew tells that Moses appeared with Elias, talking with Jesus on the mountain.",
            "refs": [
              [
                4020001,
                4020001
              ],
              [
                4020008,
                4020013
              ],
              [
                4020025,
                4020028
              ],
              [
                5003023,
                5003027
              ],
              [
                5034001,
                5034008
              ],
              [
                40017001,
                40017003
              ]
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ],
